  "text_md": "## CHAPTER XXIV\n\n## VARIABLES AND LIMITS\n\n400. Functions (§ 292)  are usually  denoted by  symbols  of the  form  f(x),  P(@),  F(@),  etc.,  and are read  f  function of a, P function of x,  etc.\n\nThus,  f(x')  comprises all  expressions  which  involve  the  letter  «,  and  it\n\nmay  represent in  one  discussion 322+ 2+  1, in another  Vz, in a third 4x  + 2%3,  etc.\n\n401. If f(x)  is  known in  any  particular  discussion,  f(a)  is\n2. formed  by substituting  a  in  place  of a.\n\n<!-- formula-not-decoded -->\n\n402. If  y= f(x),  «  is  called the independent  variable,  and  y the  dependent  variable.\n403. Similarly,  /(#, 7) denotes  a function  of two independent variables. Thus, 2?+3ay+y', or Va+vVy,  or zi may be represented by the  symbol  /(a, y). y\n\n<!-- formula-not-decoded -->\n\n## EXERCISE 142\n\n1. If f(x) =2?+3e+42,  find  f(1), f0),  f(-1).\n\n<!-- formula-not-decoded -->\n\n<!-- formula-not-decoded -->",
